  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Linking Pregnant Women to Maternal Health Services</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Large numbers of Nigerian women continue to die in childbirth, despite improvements in the last decade. In north eastern Nigeria, transport options for pregnant women to reach skilled health care are limited by distance, cost and lack of ambulances.  Only 11% of women in Adamawa state deliver in health facilities compared to 38% nationally.  This project will mobilise taxis to provide cheap and easy-to-reach emergency transport, with a priority for pregnant women with complications.  The project will sensitise and train drivers across Adamawa state, and enable the National Union of Road Transport Workers (NURTW) to supervise the activities. The project will build on previous success in several other states with a similar Emergency Transport Scheme.  As a result, there will be a greater uptake of maternal health services, which will reduce the number of maternal deaths.</narrative>
  </description>
